---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import SkillBlock from '../components/SkillBlock.astro';
import DemoPlayer from '../components/DemoPlayer.astro';
import KeepGoing from '../components/KeepGoing.astro';
---
<Base
  title="Context hygiene · agent-toolkit"
  description="See what auto-loads into your agent before you even type, and trim it without breaking anything."
  back
>
  <PageHeader title="Context hygiene skills" heading="Your context is often cluttered before you even type">
    <p class="text-muted mb-3">
      AI agents work at their best when the context window is lean. Reasoning is sharpest in the
      first part of the window and degrades well before the hard limit, so every token you load
      has to earn its place. The smaller the context, the sharper the agent.
    </p>
    <p class="text-muted">
      Much of that context is spent before you even type: governing docs, skills, MCP servers
      and auto-memory all load at startup. These two skills tackle the two places where clutter
      builds up: your setup and your agent's memory.
    </p>
  </PageHeader>
  <SkillBlock name="context-checkup" color="blue"
    article="https://medium.com/engineering-in-the-age-of-ai/keep-your-ai-agents-context-window-sharp-7255d83a8949"
    articleLabel="Read more about the context window"
  >
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Audits everything that auto-loads at startup: the governing-doc chain, skill descriptions,
      MCP servers, memory. It measures each source instead of guessing, ranks the trims by payoff
      and keeps every change reversible. Nothing is touched without your approval.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="05-context-checkup" cols={72} rows={16} label="context-checkup proposing a trim" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <SkillBlock name="memory-doctor" color="blue"
    article="https://medium.com/engineering-in-the-age-of-ai/keep-your-ai-agents-memory-clean-and-organized-with-memory-doctor-a79f7174f257"
    articleLabel="Read more about memory-doctor"
  >
    <p class="text-muted mb-4">
      Auto-memory is a hidden side-channel into every session's context: facts pile up there
      that you never review. This skill drains it block by block, relocating each entry to a
      home you control (a doc, a skill, a rule) or archiving it. You confirm every action.
      Memory works best as an inbox, not a filing cabinet: entries land, get reviewed and move
      on.
    </p>
    <DemoPlayer cast="16-memory-doctor" cols={72} rows={11} label="memory-doctor relocating a block" />
  </SkillBlock>
  <KeepGoing current="context-hygiene" />
</Base>
